From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Burke Cates <burke.cates@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 23:10:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] NOT-FOR-UPSTREAM: rtc: qcom-glink: use the SoCCP GIO time interface

The SoCCP firmware on production Glymur laptops (ASUS Zenbook A16
UX3607OA, BIOS 312) does not implement the requests this driver sends.
Its pmic_glink owner 0x8010 only serves the ACPI Time and Alarm Device
(TAD) wake timer requests 0x60-0x65 and does not reply to anything
else, so GET_RTC_TICKS (0x67) and GET_REAL_TIME (0x68) time out. Worse,
0x63 is TAD _STV and 0x65 is TAD _TIV there: SET_PROPERTY arms a wake
timer and GET_PROPERTY reads one back.

The firmware keeps the real time in its generic I/O (GIO) service on
owner 0x8011 instead. Opcode 0x90 reads and 0x91 writes a block of the
firmware's GIO address space, and the 16-byte TAD real time buffer is
at 0x30200. The firmware rejects address/length pairs it doesn't know.

Use that. The buffer holds local time plus the TimeZone offset (in
minutes, daylight saving included), so read_time() returns the time
plus TimeZone, i.e. UTC. Its Valid byte reads 0 even for a good time,
so it is ignored. When the RTC loses power the firmware reports
2025-01-06 without a time zone; read_time() rejects that and anything
before 2000.

As with rtc-pm8xxx, set_time() is only offered when the pmic-glink node
has allow-set-time, so that otherwise the NTP sync never writes the
firmware's clock. It writes UTC with TimeZone 0, Daylight 0 and Valid
0, which reads back as UTC. The firmware's write status is non-zero
even for writes that took, so ignore it and read the time back instead.

Drop the alarm support and the wakeup capability for now: the alarm
requests went to TAD _STV/_TIV with the wrong arguments, and the
firmware's alarm notification has not been worked out.

